      "worked": "Assume $A\\ne B$. The centre is the midpoint $M$ of $AB$, and the radius is half the length of $AB$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}M&=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right),\\\\r^2&=\\frac{(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2}{4}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe circle therefore has equation\n\n$$\\left(x-\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(y-\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right)^2=\\frac{(x_1-x_2)^2+(y_1-y_2)^2}{4}.$$\n\nFor the $x$-terms,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(x-\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{(x_1-x_2)^2}{4}&=x^2-(x_1+x_2)x+x_1x_2,\\\\\\left(x-\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{(x_1-x_2)^2}{4}&=(x-x_1)(x-x_2).\\end{align*}$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\\left(y-\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{(y_1-y_2)^2}{4}=(y-y_1)(y-y_2).$$\n\nSubtracting the squared-radius terms from the circle equation gives\n\n$$(x-x_1)(x-x_2)+(y-y_1)(y-y_2)=0.$$\n\nThese equivalent equations describe the full circle, including both diameter endpoints. If $A=B$, the equation becomes $(x-x_1)^2+(y-y_1)^2=0$, which represents only the point $A$, a degenerate circle.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
